  "content": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "title": "Derived Stems"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Arabic verbs are noted for an unusual system of derivation. From any particular root various verb stems may be formed. Western scholars usually refer to these derivations as “form I”, “form II”, ... up through “form XV,” though these designations are not used indigenously, where they are referred to by derivations from the root "
        },
        { "type": "ref", "href": "/root/فعل", "arabic": "ف ع ل", "transliteration": "f ʕ l" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ". Accordingly, form I would be " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَعَلَ", "href": null, "transliteration": "faʕala" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", form II would be " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَعَّلَ", "href": null, "transliteration": "faʕʕala" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": ", etc. These forms refer to triliteral roots (those made of three consonants). There are also quadriliteral roots, made up of four consonants, which come in four forms, “form Iq”, “form IIq”, “form IIIq” and “form IVq”. Triliteral forms XI through XV and quadriliteral forms IIIq and IVq are rare and tend to be intransitive, often stative, verbs (having the meaning “to be or become Y” where Y is an adjective)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "These forms and their associated participles and verbal nouns are the primary means of forming vocabulary in Arabic. All of the examples shown here are the citation forms, which in Arabic means the 3rd-person masculine singular perfect (e.g., “he did”, “he wrote”)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "title": "Form I"
    },
    {
      "type": "mainCategory",
      "href": "arabic-verbs/form/1",
      "label": "Arabic form-I verbs"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "value": "Perfective " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَعَلَ", "href": null, "transliteration": "faʕala" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَعِلَ", "href": null, "transliteration": "faʕila" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَعُلَ", "href": null, "transliteration": "faʕula" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", imperfective " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "يَفْعَلُ", "href": null, "transliteration": "yafʕalu" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "يَفْعِلُ", "href": null, "transliteration": "yafʕilu" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "يَفْعُلُ", "href": null, "transliteration": "yafʕulu" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", active participle " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "فَاعِل", "href": null, "transliteration": "fāʕil" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", passive participle " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "مَفْعُول", "href": null, "transliteration": "mafʕūl" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", imperative (2nd person, m, sg) " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "اِفْعَلْ", "href": null, "transliteration": "ifʕal" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "اِفْعِلْ", "href": null, "transliteration": "ifʕil" },
        { "type": "text", "value": ", " },
        { "type": "ref", "arabic": "اُفْعُلْ", "href": null, "transliteration": "ufʕul" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "This is the simplest basic form of a verb; it gives the general idea of its root. Most verbs are triliteral, but there are a few quadriliteral ones."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
